{"name":"Japanese Laurel","hex":"#2f7532","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"laurel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen shrub of species Laurus nobilis, having aromatic leaves of a lanceolate shape, with clusters of small, yellowish white flowers in their axils.","A crown of laurel."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with laurel, especially with a laurel wreath.","To enwreathe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/laurel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aucuba japonica","blocks":[{"text":"Aucuba japonica, commonly called spotted laurel, Japanese laurel, Japanese aucuba or gold dust plant (U.S.), is a shrub native to rich forest soils of moist valleys, thickets, by streams and near shaded moist rocks in China, Korea, and Japan. This is the species of Aucuba commonly seen in gardens - often in variegated form. The leaves are opposite, broad lanceolate, 5–8 cm (2.0–3.1 in) long and 2–5 cm (0.79–1.97 in) wide. Aucuba japonica are dioecious. The flowers are small, 4–8 mm (0.16–0.31 in) diameter, each with four purplish-brown petals; they are produced in clusters of 10-30 in a loose cyme. The fruit is a red drupe approximately 1 cm (0.39 in) in diameter that is avoided by birds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Aucuba_japonica_Gold_Dust_NBG_LR.jpg/330px-Aucuba_japonica_Gold_Dust_NBG_LR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aucuba_japonica","wikidata":"Q163114"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/WitteHeinrichFlora1868-003-Aucuba_japonica.png/120px-WitteHeinrichFlora1868-003-Aucuba_japonica.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/WitteHeinrichFlora1868-003-Aucuba_japonica.png/250px-WitteHeinrichFlora1868-003-Aucuba_japonica.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p